STEP 1
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
---                                                                                                                  
  The Alpha-Beta Communication Cost Model                                                                              
                                                                                                                       
  The alpha-beta model (T = α + β·n) is the standard way to model point-to-point communication cost in distributed     
  systems:                                                                                                             
  
  ┌──────────┬───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   
  │  Symbol  │     Name      │                                       Meaning                                       │
  ├──────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ α        │ Latency       │ Fixed startup cost to send any message, regardless of size (microseconds). Captures │
  │ (alpha)  │               │  driver overhead, PCIe/NVLink handshake, kernel launch latency.                     │
  ├──────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   
  │ β (beta) │ Inverse       │ Time per byte = 1 / bandwidth. A lower β means higher bandwidth (e.g., NVLink β ≈   │
  │          │ bandwidth     │ 0.003 µs/byte vs InfiniBand β ≈ 0.03 µs/byte).                                      │   
  └──────────┴───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                                                                                       
  So the time to transfer n bytes between two GPUs = α + β × n.                                                        
  
  ---                                                                                                                  
  What mesh_alpha and mesh_beta Are
                                                                                                                       
  They are 2-D matrices — one entry per GPU pair:
                                                                                                                       
  mesh_alpha[i][j] = measured α (latency, µs) for the link GPU_i → GPU_j
  mesh_beta[i][j]  = measured β (inverse bandwidth, µs/byte) for the link GPU_i → GPU_j                                
                                                                                                                       
  Because not all GPU links are equal:                                                                                 
  - Same node, NVLink: low α (~5 µs), very low β (~0.003 µs/byte → ~333 GB/s)                                          
  - Cross-node, InfiniBand: higher α (~20 µs), higher β (~0.03 µs/byte → ~33 GB/s)                                     
                                                                                  
  The AlphaBetaProfiler actually sends probe tensors of varying sizes between every GPU pair and fits the measured     
  times to T = α + β·n to extract real numbers — not textbook estimates.


