  STEP 6:
  ----------------------------------------------------------------
  ---                                                                                                                  
  Step 6: initialize_model() for This Rank's Stage Only                                                                
                                                                                                                       
  This is where planning ends and the real, runnable model begins. It takes this rank's pipeline stage layers and      
  produces a fully TP+DP sharded ModuleWrapper ready for the training loop.                                            
                  
  ---                                                                                                                  
  What It Receives
                                                                                                                       
  stage_start, stage_end = plan.stage_layer_ranges[current_stage]
  stage_layers  = layers[stage_start:stage_end]   # e.g. layers[2:4] for stage 1                                       
  stage_module  = _StageModule(stage_layers)       # wraps them into one nn.Module                                     
  stage_wrapped = initialize_model(                                                                                    
      model       = stage_module,                                                                                      
      meta_args   = meta_args,          # tensor shapes (no real data)                                                 
      device_mesh = stage_device_mesh,  # this rank's (tp × dp) mesh from Step 5                                       
      memory_budget = memory_budget,                                                                                   
      ...                                                                                                              
  )                                                                                                                    
                  
  Each rank only processes its own stage's layers. Stage 0 sees layers 0–1, stage 1 sees layers 2–3, etc.              
   
  ---                                                                                                                  
  Inside initialize_model() — 4 sub-steps
                                                                                                                       
  Sub-step A: FX Tracing — build the compute graph
                                                                                                                       
  tracer = ColoTracer(trace_act_ckpt=True, bias_addition_split=True)
  graph = tracer.trace(root=model, meta_args=meta_args)                                                                
  graph.set_codegen(ActivationCheckpointCodeGen())                                                                     
  gm = ColoGraphModule(model, graph, ...)                                                                              
  shape_prop_pass(gm, *meta_args.values())                                                                             
  gm.recompile()                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                       
  - ColoTracer runs symbolic tracing using meta_args (fake tensors of the right shape, no actual data, no GPU memory) —
   it traces the entire forward pass as a static computation graph of torch.fx nodes                                   
  - bias_addition_split=True — splits y = x @ W + b into two separate nodes so the ILP can shard each independently    
  - trace_act_ckpt=True — preserves activation checkpoint regions in the graph                                         
  - shape_prop_pass — annotates every node with its output tensor shape                                                
  - gm.recompile() — regenerates Python source for the graph module                                                    
                                                                                                                       
  Result: a ColoGraphModule — a torch.fx.GraphModule with shape annotations on every node.                             
                                                                                                                       
  ---                                                                                                                  
  Sub-step B: build_strategy_constructor() — enumerate sharding strategies per operator
                                                                                                                       
  strategies_constructor = build_strategy_constructor(
      graph, device_mesh, solver_preference, dataloader_option, shard_option                                           
  )                                                                                                                    
  # → strategies_constructor.build_strategies_and_cost()                                                               
                                                                                                                       
  For every node in the graph (matmul, LayerNorm, attention, etc.), a node handler generates all valid sharding        
  strategies and their costs:                                                                                          
                                                                                                                       
  node: linear (hidden_states @ W)
  strategies:                                                                                                          
    [0] replicate input, replicate weight → no comm, full memory
    [1] shard input on batch, replicate weight → all-reduce output                                                     
    [2] shard weight on col (col-parallel) → all-reduce output                                                         
    [3] shard weight on row (row-parallel) → reduce-scatter input                                                      
    ...                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                       
  Each strategy has:                                                                                                   
  - A ShardingSpec describing how the tensor is distributed across device_mesh's axes
  - A compute cost (FLOPs estimate)                                                                                    
  - A communication cost (using α/β from device_mesh.mesh_alpha / mesh_beta)
  - A memory cost (activations + parameters on this device)                                                            
                                                                                                                       
  ---
  Sub-step C: solve_solution() — ILP finds the globally optimal strategy assignment                                    
                                                                                                                       
  solution, objective = solve_solution(gm, strategies_constructor, memory_budget)
                                                                                                                       
  Internally:     
  1. CostGraph — builds an edge-weighted graph where nodes are operators and edges represent resharding costs between
  adjacent operators (e.g. if node A outputs column-sharded but node B expects row-sharded, a reshard is needed)       
  2. cost_graph.simplify_graph() — merges linear chains to reduce ILP size                                      
  3. Solver.call_solver_serialized_args() — calls PuLP/COIN-BC ILP solver:                                             
  minimize: Σ compute_cost[i, solution[i]]                                                                             
          + Σ comm_cost[i, solution[i]]                                                                                
          + Σ reshard_cost[i→j, solution[i], solution[j]]                                                              
  subject to: memory[i, solution[i]] ≤ memory_budget  (per device)                                                     
                                                                                                                       
  Result: solution = list of integers, one per graph node:                                                             
  solution[0] = 2   → node 0 (embed): use strategy 2 (shard on vocab axis)                                             
  solution[1] = 1   → node 1 (attn_q): use strategy 1 (col-parallel)                                                   
  solution[2] = 3   → node 2 (attn_v): use strategy 3 (row-parallel)                                                   
  ...                                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                       
  ---                                                                                                                  
  Sub-step D: transform_to_sharded_model() — rewrite the graph to execute the plan
                                                                                                                       
  gm, sharding_spec_dicts = transform_to_sharded_model(
      gm, meta_args, solution, device_mesh, strategies_constructor                                                     
  )               
                                                                                                                       
  Two passes rewrite the graph:                                                                                        
   
  runtime_preparation_pass:                                                                                            
  - Shards the actual parameter tensors — W matrices are sliced and distributed across GPUs according to solution[i]'s
  ShardingSpec                                                                                                         
  - Inserts grad hooks for DP gradient all-reduce
  - Records sharding_spec_dict (target input specs per node), origin_spec_dict (output specs), comm_actions_dict (what 
  collectives to run and when)                                                                                         
                                                                                                                       
  runtime_apply_pass:                                                                                                  
  - Inserts explicit communication nodes into the graph at points where a reshard is needed (e.g. all-gather,          
  all-reduce, reduce-scatter)                                                                                          
  - These become real dist.all_reduce() / dist.all_gather() calls at runtime
                                                                                                                       
  After gm.recompile(), the graph module's Python code now contains both the compute ops and the communication ops     
  inline.                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                       
  ---                                                                                                                  
  The Returned ModuleWrapper

  ModuleWrapper(gm, sharding_spec_dict, origin_spec_dict, comm_actions_dict)

  It's a thin nn.Module wrapper that calls gm.forward() with the three spec dicts passed in. This means forward() is   
  just:
                                                                                                                       
  def forward(self, *args, **kwargs):
      return self.module(
          *args,
          sharding_spec_convert_dict = self.sharding_spec_dict,
          origin_node_sharding_spec_dict = self.origin_spec_dict,
          comm_actions_dict = self.comm_actions_dict,                                                                  
          **kwargs,
      )                                                                                                                
                  
  The recompiled graph module uses those dicts at runtime to trigger the right resharding collectives at the right     
  points in the forward pass.
                                                                                                                       
  ---             
  End-to-end data flow for Step 6
                                                                                                                       
  layers[stage_start:stage_end]        ← this rank's slice of the model
          │                                                                                                            
          ▼       
  ColoTracer.trace()                   ← symbolic FX graph (no real data)                                              
          │                                                                                                            
          ▼
  build_strategy_constructor()         ← per-node strategy list + costs                                                
          │                               using device_mesh α/β                                                        
          ▼                                                                                                            
  solve_solution() [ILP]               ← solution[i] = best strategy per node                                          
          │                                                                                                            
          ▼       
  runtime_preparation_pass()           ← shard W matrices, add grad hooks                                              
  runtime_apply_pass()                 ← inject allreduce/allgather nodes
  gm.recompile()                       ← regenerate Python from new graph                                              
          │       
          ▼                                                                                                            
  ModuleWrapper(gm, spec_dicts)        ← ready-to-train nn.Module
                                          TP+DP sharded, comms baked in                                                
                                                                                                                       
  Every rank returns its own stage_wrapped — a different slice of the model, sharded differently — and these connect   
  together via the PipelineStageManager P2P send/recv from Step 4 to form the full 3D-parallel training pipeline.  